# name-resolution-is-linear-in-stack-depth — a program got slower the deeper it ran

STATUS: DONE in 2.0.13. Found 2026-08-29 by profiling before opening the
next compiler slice; fixed the same day.

## Symptom

Every function call and every global-variable read cost O(call-stack
depth). The SAME loop, run forty frames down, took 2.7x longer than at
the top level — measured end to end, so on work alone (startup removed)
nearer 3.7x. Nothing in the program changed; only where it was called
from.

    ;; identical body, called at depth 1 and at depth 40
    (defun work (n / i s)
      (setq i 0 s 0)
      (while (< i n) (setq s (+ i 1)) (setq i (1+ i)))
      s)
    (defun deep (k n) (if (< k 1) (work n) (deep (- k 1) n)))

    clautolisp -O3 -l shallow.lsp    236 ms
    clautolisp -O3 -l deep.lsp       578 ms

This is the shape of bug that never shows up in a micro-benchmark,
because a micro-benchmark runs at depth 1. It shows up in an
application, which is nested — and it shows up as "clautolisp is slow",
with nothing to point at.

## Cause

LOOKUP-FUNCTION and FIND-DYNAMIC-BINDING (which LOOKUP-VARIABLE,
SET-VARIABLE and LOOKUP-DOCUMENTATION all go through) walked the WHOLE
dynamic-frame chain, doing an EQ ASSOC in each frame, before falling
back to the namespace.

That walk exists for a real reason: AutoLISP is a lisp-1, so a parameter
or a `/'-local can shadow a function name, and the rule is documented and
tested. But for a function name the walk essentially always FAILS — `+'
is not a parameter of anything — and failing costs one ASSOC per live
frame. So the cost of resolving `+' was the depth of the stack.

The profile said so plainly, and said it about a workload that had
nothing to do with shadowing (compiled fib 30):

    FRAME-BINDING       18.7% self   31.7% total
    LOOKUP-FUNCTION     17.6% self   52.1% total
    %ASSOC-EQ           14.2% self
    ...
    BUILTIN--            2.4%
    BUILTIN-+            1.1%

Over half the time resolving names; about 4% doing the arithmetic the
program was written to do.

## Fix

Each AUTOLISP-SYMBOL carries DYNAMIC-BINDING-COUNT: how many live frames
bind it. When it is zero, no frame binds the symbol, so the walk is
guaranteed to fail and is skipped outright.

It is a count, not a cache and not a heuristic — zero is an exact
statement — and it moves in exactly two places, which is what makes it
checkable:

  - (SETF FRAME-BINDING) increments, but only for a NEW entry; replacing
    the binding of a symbol the frame already binds does not, because
    the frame still binds it once.
  - POP-DYNAMIC-FRAME decrements for every symbol the popped frame held.

Those two are the only ways a frame gains or loses a binding: nothing
outside PUSH/POP-DYNAMIC-FRAME writes the context's frame slot, every
push is paired with a pop under UNWIND-PROTECT, and every binding
installer in the tree funnels through BIND-DYNAMIC-VARIABLE into (SETF
FRAME-BINDING). The debugger only reads the chain.

DIRECTION OF ERROR, deliberately: over-counting costs speed and nothing
else — the walk simply happens, as before. Under-counting would be a
correctness bug. So nothing clamps: a count driven negative by some
future mistake stays negative, fails the ZEROP test, and walks.

## Result

Best of five, built SBCL image, same machine:

                        before    after   speedup
    -O0 fib 27          781 ms   515 ms    1.52x
    -O3 fib 27          619 ms   358 ms    1.73x
    -O0 loop, depth 1   367 ms   366 ms    1.00x
    -O3 loop, depth 1   236 ms   238 ms    1.00x
    -O0 loop, depth 40  707 ms   364 ms    1.94x
    -O3 loop, depth 40  578 ms   232 ms    2.49x

The depth-1 rows are the control, and they are the most informative
rows in the table: they are UNCHANGED. There was nothing to skip at
depth 1, so nothing got faster — which is what says the win comes from
removing the depth walk and not from something else having shifted.

And the loop is now depth-invariant: 232 ms at depth 40 against 238 ms
at depth 1. That property, rather than any single number, is the fix.

The interpreter gets it too (1.5-1.9x): this is runtime, not compiler.

## Tests

The tests assert the INVARIANT that licenses the skip, not the timing it
buys — a count is checkable and a stopwatch on a shared machine is not:
a called name's count is zero; a parameter's count is one per live frame
and returns to zero; an error unwinding out of a call still releases it;
a `/'-local really does shadow a global function; a non-callable shadow
stays transparent; a symbol bound twice in one frame counts once.

The skip was verified LOAD-BEARING by breaking it: forcing
SYMBOL-HAS-NO-DYNAMIC-BINDING-P to always answer T fails 31 tests across
the existing corpus — shadowing, LET teardown, documentation scoping.
The walk is guarded by far more than the new tests.

## Where the time goes now

Re-profiled the same workload after the fix. LOOKUP-FUNCTION 52% -> 15%;
FRAME-BINDING and %ASSOC-EQ are gone from the profile entirely. What is
now on top is the CALL PROTOCOL itself:

    CALL-AUTOLISP-FUNCTION-IN-CONTEXT  20.7% self
    WRAP-BUILTIN-FUNCTION's &rest lambda 7.4% self / 25.1% total
    LOOKUP-FUNCTION                     5.9% self
    the arithmetic                     ~10% all together

So the next slice is the per-call cost — the &rest argument list, the
call-stack cons, the HANDLER-CASE established per builtin call — and NOT
open-coding operators, which the standing plan named and which this
measurement puts at about a tenth of the time. Recorded in
compiler.issue.
